  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nA cold front has sagged south and stalled out over central WI. Radar \nis showing some weak echoes moving across northern MN with a few \nsurface observations indicating pockets of light rainfall are making \nit to the surface. This activity is expected to push northeast \nthrough the morning hours with a little lull in activity \nthrough midday. Heading into the latter half of the day we will \nsee a strong shortwave advance out of the Northern Plains \nboosting synoptic forcing and bringing another round of more \nwidespread rainfall. Farther south, closer to the surface low \nmoving across southern MN, we will see some increased MUCAPE \nwhich will lead to a brief window for some isolated \nthunderstorms to develop from the Brainerd Lakes region into NW \nWI. Steep mid level lapse rates from the influx of cool air \naloft paired with ample amounts of bulk shear could lead to \nsmall hail in some of the more robust cells. Activity will taper\noff overnight with rain totals ranging from 0.10-0.35\"\n\nTuesday and Wednesday:\n\nRain exits to the east through Tuesday morning with high \npressure setting up residence over the region. Temperatures will\nbe much more seasonable with highs in the 50s and 60s. \nOvernight lows will fall back into the 30s. No precipitation \nexpected through this period. \n\nThursday and Friday:\n\nThe 00Z suite of guidance is starting to come into better agreement \nwith the overall pattern for late into the work week. Two lows will \nbe the primary players with the first low ejecting out of the \nCentral Rockies starting Thursday morning. Another low is projected \nto be dropping south out of Saskatchewan. There is initially a \ndry sector in between these features that will be over the \nNorthern Plains and the Upper Midwest that will need to be \noverrun in order for rain to transpire. But with the pincer \nlike movement of these features into the Northland rain chances \nwill likely increase. Placement and timing of these lows are \nstill a bit contested among ensemble guidance which will greatly\ninfluence on whether or not the southern low can tap into some \nGulf moisture as it moves across the Midwest. \n\nWeekend:\n\nThe aforementioned lows will merge over the Great Lakes leading to a \nstrong pressure gradient setting up over the Northland. Gusty north \nwinds with ample cold air pouring in from the north will lead to a \nvery chilly Saturday morning with lows in the 20s. Dry air will also \ndominant most the region leading to mostly sunny skies and \nhighs continuing to be in the 50s and 60s. The dry and windy \nconditions may prompt fire weather concerns for Saturday.",
